      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 709,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 472,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107287,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 181,
        "headingId": 472,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>These RCCs have no specific clinical features, aside from the association of <em>VCL</em>::<em>ALK</em> RCC with young patients who have sickle cell trait (see below).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T10:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 710,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 473,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107288,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 181,
        "headingId": 473,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>There have been approximately 40&nbsp;reported cases, with a nearly equal sex distribution, in both children and adults (patient age range: 3&ndash;85&nbsp;years).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T10:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 711,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 474,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107289,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 181,
        "headingId": 474,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T10:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 712,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 475,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107290,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 181,
        "headingId": 475,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T10:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 719,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 482,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107291,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 181,
        "headingId": 482,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Most of the tumours have been small and organ-confined (pT1) at presentation.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T10:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 713,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 476,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107292,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 181,
        "headingId": 476,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The 4&nbsp;known cases with a <em>VCL</em>::<em>ALK</em> gene fusion (3&nbsp;reported) are distinctive in that they affected young patients with sickle cell trait and have a distinctive morphology. These neoplasms are typically well circumscribed and may have chronic inflammation at their periphery. They are composed of polygonal neoplastic cells with abundant eosinophilic cytoplasm and striking vacuolization {21076462; 21213368; 24698962}. Sickle-shaped erythrocytes are more readily seen in extravasated blood than in the blood vessels. These neoplasms have been proposed to be the eighth sickle cell nephropathy {24698962}. <em>ALK</em>-rearranged RCCs with fusion partners other than <em>VCL</em>&nbsp;&ndash; such as <em>TPM3</em>, <em>EML4</em>, <em>STRN</em>, and <em>HOOK1</em>&nbsp;&ndash; are more heterogeneous {22252991; 22743654; 26848800; 27225638; 32467651}. They may show cytoplasmic vacuolization but often have a papillary or cribriform architecture and mucinous stroma. Other morphologies resembling metanephric adenoma or mucinous tubular and spindle cell carcinoma have also been reported {32467651}. By immunohistochemistry, ALK labelling is a useful screening tool for these neoplasms. SMARCB1 protein (also known as INI1, SNF5, or BAF47), which is typically lost in the renal medullary carcinomas that affect patients with sickle cell trait, is retained. TFE3 immunoreactivity is frequently seen using automated staining techniques, but it is usually negative in more standard overnight incubation procedures, and <em>TFE3</em> gene rearrangements are not present.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T10:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 714,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 477,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107293,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 181,
        "headingId": 477,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T10:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 715,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 478,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107294,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 181,
        "headingId": 478,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Demonstration of <em>ALK</em> rearrangement with various partners is required for the diagnosis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T10:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 718,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 481,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107295,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 181,
        "headingId": 481,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> RCC with documentation of <em>ALK</em> rearrangement by break-apart FISH or demonstration of an <em>ALK</em> fusion using sequencing methods.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> ALK immunoreactivity.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T10:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 716,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 479,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107296,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 181,
        "headingId": 479,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Union for International Cancer Control (UICC) TNM RCC tumour staging is used.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T10:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 717,
    "bookid": 44,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 480,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 107297,
        "bookId": 44,
        "chapterId": 181,
        "headingId": 480,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Follow-up is limited in the reported cases. Ten patients had no evidence of disease in limited follow-up. Two adults died of disease, and three other patients had lymph node metastases or local recurrence. Three patients with metastatic disease had dramatic responses to a targeted ALK inhibitor, indicating that ALK inhibition is a non-toxic and potentially highly effective treatment in these patients {29685646}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2023-03-09T10:33:35"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]